A||||4||Gold-86SE||84 Duke, loss of water, over heating, help!||||9||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||06-22-2000||02:38 PM||||<BR>Here's the deal: (btw, sorry for the length)<BR>This little '84 Duke has been running good for me, but it has been losing coolant. I have to check the coolant level every time before I drive it.<P>I thought it was possibly leaking from the gaskets around the water pump, because it had sat for a long time, at least a year. Nevertheless, I replaced the water pump, gaskets, coolant hoses (front and back), and flushed out the radiator, but I did not replace the heater hoses.<P>I have been driving the thing over the last couple of days, and have been adding water, thinking it was due to burping the system.  However, one would think that the system would have been finished by now.<P>Upon inspection, I have not noticed any leaking coolant from the areas on which I fixed, yet near the water pump I have heard hissing and have the smell of anti-freeze.<P>I had this problem prior to the recent work, but I chalked it up, at that time, to bad gaskets around the water pump, yet the problem still remains.<P>The recall work was supposedly done, so what else can I check regarding this problem? Do I have a cracked head? Bad head bolts? What gives? Help.<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||06-22-2000||02:49 PM||||Ive been losing water also. It was coming form the thermostat housing. I guess they warp over time (There was a VERY small leak there, I could barley notice it). The dealer still sells a replacement housing (Improved with rubber gasket built in) Its about $45.00<P>But that was just my problem  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||5
Z||000002||Gold-86SE||06-22-2000||02:57 PM||||<BR>Haze, that is a thought. I had not consider that area of the block. However, one question, why would the thermostat housing cause the hissing and smell to eminate from the area of the water pump?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||06-22-2000||05:00 PM||||Tiny coolant leaks can be hard to find. They can leak at one point and travel along then reappear somewhere else. Check all your hoses. On the DIS Dukes there is a small coolant line under the intake manifold that sometimes leaks, especially at the coupler . Don't know if the 84 models had that same coolant line too or not. If you suspect a bad head gasket, see if you have coolant in your oil and vice versa. Do a compression check. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||06-22-2000||09:20 PM||||If you do find a head leak, REPLACE ALL the head bolts. Never ever reuse 4 cyl head bolts. Even if the recal work was done. (The recall doesn't replace all the weak bolts.)||||reg||1
